      "content": "That's a good start, but it's a bit broad. To make my essay really strong, I need to get into the actual \"logic\" they used. For example, when they talked about \"perceived differences in social norms or temperaments,\" how did they actually defend that? I'm looking for the specific pseudo-scientific or sociological reasoning they used to claim that integration would cause \"societal friction.\" If I can understand the specific \"logic\" of those arguments—even the parts that look like social Darwinism—I can better explain why they thought it was a stabilizer at the time.",
